---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GuideArticle from "../../components/GuideArticle.astro";
import ToolCTA from "../../components/ToolCTA.astro";
import RelatedTools from "../../components/RelatedTools.astro";
import RelatedGuides from "../../components/RelatedGuides.astro";
import LandlordMathLink from "../../components/LandlordMathLink.astro";
import FAQBlock from "../../components/FAQBlock.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";
import { site } from "../../data/site";

const guide = getGuide("rent-price-per-square-foot")!;
const guideUrl = `${site.url}/guides/${guide.slug}/`;
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Is higher price per square foot always better?",
    answer: "No. It depends on property type, market demand, condition, and how the rental compares with similar properties."
  },
  {
    question: "Why are smaller units more expensive per square foot?",
    answer: "Smaller units often have higher demand and use space more efficiently, so the total rent can be lower while the price per square foot is higher."
  },
  {
    question: "Should I price at the top of the range?",
    answer: "Only if your property stands out. Strong condition, layout, location, or included amenities may support a premium, but pricing too high can increase vacancy risk."
  },
  {
    question: "How many properties should I compare?",
    answer: "Compare at least 3 to 5 similar rentals when possible. The closer they are in size, type, condition, and location, the more useful the comparison will be."
  }
];
const structuredData = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: guide.h1,
    description: guide.description,
    url: guideUrl,
    mainEntityOfPage: guideUrl,
    isPartOf: {
      "@type": "WebSite",
      name: site.name,
      url: site.url
    }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.url },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: `${site.url}/guides/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: guide.pageTitle, item: guideUrl }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: faq.question,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: faq.answer
      }
    }))
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={guide.title} description={guide.description} structuredData={structuredData}>
  <GuideArticle guide={guide}>
    <section class="guide-section">
      <p>When trying to estimate rent, one of the most useful metrics is price per square foot.</p>
      <p>It gives you a simple way to compare properties, even when they are different sizes.</p>
      <p>But there is no single correct number. It depends entirely on your market, property type, and condition.</p>
      <p>This guide shows you how to use price per square foot correctly and how to connect it to the calculators on this site.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>What Is Price Per Square Foot?</h2>
      <p>Price per square foot is the monthly rent divided by the property's square footage.</p>
      <p><strong>Price per square foot = Rent / Square footage</strong></p>
      <p>Example:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rent: $1,800</li>
        <li>Size: 1,200 sq ft</li>
        <li>Result: $1.50 per sq ft</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This helps you compare properties of different sizes more fairly.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Why Price Per Square Foot Matters</h2>
      <p>Total rent alone can be misleading because it does not account for size.</p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Property</th>
              <th>Rent</th>
              <th>Sq Ft</th>
              <th>Price Per Sq Ft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A</td><td>$1,800</td><td>1,200</td><td>$1.50</td></tr>
            <tr><td>B</td><td>$2,000</td><td>1,500</td><td>$1.33</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Property B costs more overall, but Property A is actually more expensive per square foot.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Find Your Market Range</h2>
      <p>Do not guess a single number. Build a practical range from your property details and comparable rentals.</p>
      <ToolCTA
        title="Estimate Your Rent Range"
        text="Use the Rent Estimate Calculator to quickly generate a rent range and price per square foot based on your property details."
        button="Use the Rent Estimate Calculator"
        href="/rent-estimate/"
      />
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Compare Similar Properties</h2>
      <p>A realistic price per square foot range comes from similar rentals. Look for properties with similar size, bedroom count, bathroom count, condition, property type, and location.</p>
      <ToolCTA
        title="Compare Rentals Side by Side"
        text="Use the Rent Comparison Tool to enter multiple properties and see rent, square footage, and price per square foot together."
        button="Use the Rent Comparison Tool"
        href="/rent-comparison/"
      />
      <p>Instead of relying on one listing, look for a range such as $1.45 to $1.60 per sq ft. A tight range gives you better pricing context than a single outlier.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>What Affects Price Per Square Foot</h2>
      <h3>1. Property Size</h3>
      <p>Smaller units often have higher price per square foot. Larger properties may have higher total rent but a lower price per square foot.</p>
      <h3>2. Bedrooms and Layout</h3>
      <p>Bedroom count matters, but layout efficiency matters too. A practical layout can support stronger rent than awkward square footage.</p>
      <h3>3. Condition and Upgrades</h3>
      <p>Updated kitchens, bathrooms, flooring, paint, appliances, and lighting can increase perceived value.</p>
      <h3>4. Location</h3>
      <p>Neighborhood, commute access, nearby amenities, and local demand can move price per square foot significantly.</p>
      <h3>5. Property Type</h3>
      <p>Apartments, houses, duplexes, townhomes, and condos may rent differently even at similar sizes.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Check If Your Price Is Competitive</h2>
      <ToolCTA
        title="Check Your Market Position"
        text="Use the Market Rent Analysis Tool to see if your rent is above or below your estimated market range."
        button="Use the Market Rent Analysis Tool"
        href="/market-rent-analysis/"
      />
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Below range:</strong> the property may be underpriced.</li>
        <li><strong>Within range:</strong> the rent is likely competitive.</li>
        <li><strong>Above range:</strong> the property is using premium pricing.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>What Is a Good Price Per Square Foot?</h2>
      <p>There is no universal number. A good rent price per square foot is one that fits your local market and compares well with similar rentals.</p>
      <p>For example, if the market range is $1.45 to $1.60 per sq ft:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>$1.52 may be solid.</li>
        <li>$1.62 may be slightly premium.</li>
        <li>$1.70 may be high unless the property has clear advantages.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Using averages without context</li>
        <li>Comparing different property types</li>
        <li>Ignoring layout</li>
        <li>Overpricing based on upgrades alone</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Quick Workflow</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Use the Rent Estimate Calculator.</li>
        <li>Use the Rent Comparison Tool.</li>
        <li>Use Market Rent Analysis.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="compact-link-grid">
        <a class="related-card" href="/rent-estimate/"><strong>Rent Estimate Calculator</strong><span>Estimate rent and price per square foot.</span></a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/rent-comparison/"><strong>Rent Comparison Tool</strong><span>Compare rentals side by side.</span></a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/market-rent-analysis/"><strong>Market Rent Analysis</strong><span>Check pricing position.</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-section">
      <h2>Final Thought</h2>
      <p>Price per square foot is a powerful tool, but only when combined with comparable rentals, property condition, and market positioning.</p>
      <ToolCTA
        title="Start Estimating Rent"
        text="Use the tools below to estimate rent, compare properties, and analyze your pricing."
        button="Estimate Rent"
        href="/rent-estimate/"
      />
      <div class="action-row">
        <a class="button secondary-button" href="/rent-comparison/">Compare Rentals</a>
        <a class="button secondary-button" href="/market-rent-analysis/">Analyze Market</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </GuideArticle>

  <FAQBlock faqs={faqs} />
  <RelatedTools related={["rent-estimate", "rent-comparison", "market-rent-analysis"]} />
  <RelatedGuides related={["how-to-estimate-rent", "compare-rental-properties"]} />
  <LandlordMathLink />
</BaseLayout>
